Q1. Explain the flow of a request in Spring MVC from JSP form to Controller
    to database and back to view.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER:

The Spring MVC request flow follows these steps:

STEP 1 - User Submits JSP Form:
  -> User fills and submits a form in the browser (e.g., login.jsp).
  -> The browser sends an HTTP request (GET/POST) to the server.

STEP 2 - DispatcherServlet Receives Request:
  -> The front controller (DispatcherServlet) intercepts all incoming requests.
  -> It consults HandlerMapping to find the appropriate Controller.

STEP 3 - Controller Processes Request:
  -> The matched Controller method is called (e.g., @PostMapping("/login")).
  -> Form data is bound to model objects (e.g., @ModelAttribute User user).
  -> The controller calls the Service/DAO layer.

STEP 4 - DAO Queries Database:
  -> The DAO (using JdbcTemplate or JPA) runs a SQL query.
  -> Results are returned to the Controller as Java objects.

STEP 5 - Controller Adds Data to Model:
  -> The Controller puts results into the Model (model.addAttribute("user", user)).
  -> It returns a logical view name (e.g., "dashboard").

STEP 6 - ViewResolver Resolves View:
  -> InternalResourceViewResolver maps "dashboard" to
     /WEB-INF/views/dashboard.jsp.

STEP 7 - JSP Renders Response:
  -> The JSP accesses model data using EL (${user.name}) and renders HTML.
  -> The final HTML response is sent back to the browser.

FLOW DIAGRAM:
  Browser --> DispatcherServlet --> HandlerMapping --> Controller
                                                          |
                                                       Service
                                                          |
                                                         DAO
                                                          |
                                                       Database
                                                          |
  Browser <-- JSP (View) <-- ViewResolver <-- Model+ViewName


