Q2. Explain IoC (Inversion of Control) and Dependency Injection in Spring.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER:

IoC (Inversion of Control):
  -> IoC is a design principle where the control of object creation and
     dependency management is handed over to the Spring Container, instead
     of the developer doing it manually using "new" keyword.
  -> Without IoC: Developer creates objects manually (new UserDAO()).
  -> With IoC:    Spring Container creates and manages objects (beans).

Dependency Injection (DI):
  -> DI is the mechanism Spring uses to implement IoC.
  -> The Spring container "injects" dependencies into objects automatically.

Types of Dependency Injection:
  1. Constructor Injection  -- via class constructor
  2. Setter Injection       -- via setter methods
  3. Field Injection        -- via @Autowired on fields

Example:

  // Without DI (tight coupling):
  class LoginService {
      UserDAO dao = new UserDAO();  // manually created
  }

  // With DI (loose coupling):
  @Service
  class LoginService {
      @Autowired
      UserDAO dao;  // Spring injects this automatically
  }

Benefits of IoC and DI:
  -> Loose coupling between classes
  -> Easier unit testing (can inject mock objects)
  -> Better code maintainability


