Q6. Explain Constructor Injection vs Setter Injection.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ANSWER:

CONSTRUCTOR INJECTION:
  -> Dependencies are provided through the class constructor.
  -> The bean is fully initialized when created.

  Example:
  @Component
  class LoginService {
      private final UserDAO userDAO;

      @Autowired
      public LoginService(UserDAO userDAO) {
          this.userDAO = userDAO;
      }
  }

SETTER INJECTION:
  -> Dependencies are provided through setter methods after object creation.

  Example:
  @Component
  class LoginService {
      private UserDAO userDAO;

      @Autowired
      public void setUserDAO(UserDAO userDAO) {
          this.userDAO = userDAO;
      }
  }

COMPARISON TABLE:
  +-------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------+
  | Feature           | Constructor Injection   | Setter Injection        |
  +-------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------+
  | When injected     | At object creation      | After object creation   |
  | Null safety       | Guaranteed (not null)   | Can be null if not set  |
  | Immutability      | Supports final fields   | Cannot use final        |
  | Optional deps     | Not suitable            | Suitable                |
  | Circular deps     | Throws error            | Allowed (not ideal)     |
  | Recommended for   | Mandatory dependencies  | Optional dependencies   |
  +-------------------+-------------------------+-------------------------+

BEST PRACTICE: Spring team recommends Constructor Injection for mandatory
               dependencies.


